```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Go applications embedding wazero as a general-purpose plugin host for arbitrary third-party WASM guests (e.g., knqyf263/go-plugin with 25 NewModuleConfig sites, BARE default) that perform cryptographic operations via WASI random_get (Rust getrandom, ed25519 keygen). When the host uses wazero's default config, guests silently receive fixed-seed-42 deterministic output instead of CSPRNG entropy, violating the WASI preview1 "high-quality" + blocking contract and successor spec "cryptographically-secure" requirement.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Real win, not microbenchmark. L0 EXP-0083 demonstrates 30/30 byte-identical ed25519 keys cross-process and 10/10 identical cross-language Rust getrandom outputs under default; clock-decoupling ~31x (frozen +1ms/read, instant nanosleep). This is a security failure, not a performance artifact. However, practitioners independently hit and fixed the identical composition (CrowdSec PR#4495, wetware issue#106, wapc/wapc-go fix) without a paper, indicating the hazard is known in the wild and the synthesis validates rather than discovers a new product need.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [wazero default is documented as "deterministic source" but not as a CSPRNG demotion (config.go verbatim); changing default breaks deterministic-testing users; fix requires host opt-in WithRandSource(crypto/rand.Reader) which is one line but undiscoverable without reading source; Wasmtime and Wasmer already provide secure defaults, so wazero is the anomaly—adoption requires wazero maintainers to either change default (breaking) or add prominent warning; prevalence is signal-not-measured (7/10 curated sample, not >5% authenticated scan); market is limited to Go plugin hosts using wazero for untrusted guests, a subset of WASM runtime users]
RATIONALE: Packet cites knqyf263/go-plugin (mature host, BARE default, arbitrary-guest exposure) and wapc/wapc-go (explicit fix) as real non-strawman signal. RE-1 establishes WASI preview1 random_get "HIGH-QUALITY random data" + blocking clause and successor wasi:random "cryptographically-secure" as CSPRNG contract; Rust getrandom docs "always provides high-quality, cryptographically secure random data." RE-3 shows wazero v1.12.0 fixed-seed-42 vs Wasmtime per-context ChaCha CSPRNG (doc "Guest code may rely on this to maintain its security invariants") and Wasmer OS CSPRNG (getrandom::fill, NO deterministic path). RE-4 shows config.go documents "deterministic source... most commonly used by random_get" without warning of CSPRNG demotion. L0 EXP-0083 provides 30/30 identical ed25519 keys and 10/10 Rust getrandom identical outputs. Practitioner evidence (CrowdSec PR#4495, wetware issue#106) demonstrates independent discovery and fix, narrowing novelty to synthesis. Prevalence is bounded curated sample (7/10) not authenticated >5% measurement. Operational significance is security failure, but product need is documentation/lint, not new mechanism. Prompt version v001.
```
